  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3} & =(1)^{2}+\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{8}{3} & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{3}+{t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{4}{3}+(2)^{2}+\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+4+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
